      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we start by reducing the angle to an equivalent angle within the interval \\([0, 2\\pi)\\). We do this by subtracting multiples of \\(2\\pi\\) from \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate how many full rotations of \\(2\\pi\\) fit into \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} \\div 2\\pi = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2\\pi} = \\frac{11}{6}\n\\]\n\nThis tells us that \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3} = 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{11}{6} + \\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\). Since \\(2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{11}{6} = \\frac{22\\pi}{6} = \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) and \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{11}{6} = \\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} = 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{11}{6} + \\frac{5\\pi}{6} = 3\\pi \\cdot \\frac{11}{6} - 2\\pi + \\frac{5\\pi}{6} = 2\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\frac{5\\pi}{6} = 2\\pi + \\frac{4\\pi}{6} = 2\\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the angle \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is equivalent to \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) within the interval \\([0, 2\\pi)\\). Therefore, we need to find \\(\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nThe angle \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) is in the second quadrant, where the sine function is positive. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\pi - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{3\\pi}{3} - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe sine of the reference angle \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
